1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,525
Meet NourishNest, a household planning application that brings recipes, pantry inventory, groceries,

2
00:00:05,525 --> 00:00:11,049
and nutrition into one place. This walkthrough focuses on two things: how

3
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:16,574
AI helped us build and verify the product, and how its meal-planning

4
00:00:16,574 --> 00:00:18,876
assistant uses carefully bounded tools.

5
00:00:19,526 --> 00:00:25,717
The dashboard connects the everyday workflow. A household can manage members, browse

6
00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:31,907
recipes, track pantry stock, plan meals, and prepare grocery needs. The screens

7
00:00:31,907 --> 00:00:38,097
in this recording use synthetic demonstration data. They show the working application,

8
00:00:38,097 --> 00:00:40,677
without exposing personal household information.

9
00:00:41,327 --> 00:00:46,797
For AI-assisted development, we used Codex as a coding collaborator. Our latest

10
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:52,268
task was deliberately specific: improve image relevance and variety without redesigning the

11
00:00:52,268 --> 00:00:57,738
interface or changing backend behavior. Codex inspected the code, created a shared

12
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:03,208
image resolver, added focused tests, and used browser automation to inspect the

13
00:01:03,208 --> 00:01:08,679
result. We kept the changes reviewable and did not automatically commit or

14
00:01:08,679 --> 00:01:09,591
publish them.

15
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:15,908
Here is the concrete outcome. Images are selected by recipe identity and

16
00:01:15,908 --> 00:01:21,576
content, rather than card position. Dal maps to a legume dish, chicken

17
00:01:21,576 --> 00:01:27,244
to chicken, and khichdi to rice. Vegetarian and vegan labels exclude meat

18
00:01:27,244 --> 00:01:32,912
imagery. This demonstrates a useful AI workflow: give the coding agent a

19
00:01:32,912 --> 00:01:37,635
narrow requirement, then verify its implementation against explicit acceptance criteria.

20
00:01:38,285 --> 00:01:44,031
We used conventional engineering tools to check the AI-assisted changes. The latest

21
00:01:44,031 --> 00:01:49,778
verification recorded 141 focused tests and 613 passing tests in the full

22
00:01:49,778 --> 00:01:55,524
suite. Ruff checked the code, and browser checks covered two laptop sizes.

23
00:01:55,524 --> 00:02:01,270
These are results from this repository's verification run, not a claim that

24
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:03,665
the application is production certified.

25
00:02:04,315 --> 00:02:09,537
Now to the in-app assistant. We ask: plan one vegan dinner for

26
00:02:09,537 --> 00:02:14,759
two people within thirty minutes, and show what I need to buy.

27
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:19,981
For a reproducible walkthrough, this run uses the application's local deterministic demo

28
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:25,204
provider. It is not a live language-model response. The same assistant interface

29
00:02:25,204 --> 00:02:28,250
also supports an optional local Ollama interpreter.

30
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:34,466
The assistant makes its interpretation visible before presenting the meal preview. It

31
00:02:34,466 --> 00:02:40,033
selects from saved recipes and uses pantry information, rather than inventing meals

32
00:02:40,033 --> 00:02:45,600
or ingredients. Nutrition and grocery shortages come from the application's calculation services.

33
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:51,166
The preview does not reserve stock, place orders, or change the household's

34
00:02:51,166 --> 00:02:51,630
data.

35
00:02:52,280 --> 00:02:58,123
The assistant's tool set is deliberately small. Recommendations ranks stored recipes. Nutrition

36
00:02:58,123 --> 00:03:03,965
summary scales and totals their nutrients. Member nutrition optionally compares an adult

37
00:03:03,965 --> 00:03:09,808
profile's target. Grocery shortage calculates what remains to buy. The server controls

38
00:03:09,808 --> 00:03:15,650
the arguments and permits at most four top-level tool calls. Model output

39
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:20,519
cannot directly write to the database or decide the arithmetic.

40
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:27,262
When configured with a local model, the implemented Ollama adapter interprets natural

41
00:03:27,262 --> 00:03:33,354
language into a structured intent. Pydantic validates that intent, while deterministic Python

42
00:03:33,354 --> 00:03:39,447
services remain responsible for calculations and saved-data access. The interface is Streamlit,

43
00:03:39,447 --> 00:03:45,540
the API is FastAPI, and storage uses SQLAlchemy. Full RAG and LangGraph

44
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:51,633
multi-agent orchestration remain roadmap items; they are not part of this demonstration.

45
00:03:52,283 --> 00:03:57,574
NourishNest shows a practical approach to building with AI: use a coding

46
00:03:57,574 --> 00:04:02,865
assistant for scoped implementation, constrain the product assistant with validated tools, and

47
00:04:02,865 --> 00:04:08,156
verify the result. The goal is a useful household workflow, with clear

48
00:04:08,156 --> 00:04:11,683
evidence, visible limitations, and people staying in control.
